            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Arabic for hot, then the Arabic for crowded."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: حديثا",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "حديثا — ḥadīthan — \"recently\". Arabic's past for \"I\" ends in -تُ (-tu): قرأت حديثا كتابا جميلا — qaraʾtu ḥadīthan kitāban jamīlan — \"I recently read a lovely book.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "حديثا (ḥadīthan) is built on the native Arabic root ḥ-d-th, \"to be new; to happen\" (Lane). Ḥadīthan is ḥadīth, \"new, recent\", with the adverb ending -an."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for this chapter."
            }
          ]
        },
